WEBVTT

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.430
<v Ira Glass>Hi, everybody. I'm speaking to you today from the stage of an opera house at the Brooklyn Academy of Music in New York City.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.870
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:31.860
<v Ira Glass>A warning to listeners that there is mild cursing throughout this hour, and on the podcast, we're not going to beep that. And I'm here on an opera house stage with a story that is so small, it almost feels wrong to tell it in a room this grand. It's actually about a real opera singer-- not a super-famous opera singer or anything like that. Carin Gilfry makes about half of her living singing. The other half she makes recording audio books.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:58.300
<v Ira Glass>And about a year ago, she was staying at a hotel. And she had a deadline on this children's book that she was supposed to be recording, and the hotel room was kind of noisy. She was hearing a lot of noise from the street. And so she looked around for a quiet place, and she went into the closet-- put pillows all around to deaden the sound, sat on the floor with a microphone. And then the cord of the microphone went under the door of the closet out to her laptop, because her laptop had a fan that made noise.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:07.750
<v Ira Glass>And so she closes the door so it's pitch dark except for the light from her iPad, which had the text of the book that she was supposed to read. And she began.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:25.360
<v Carin Gilfry>The Exciting Exploits of an Effervescent Elf , written by Trisha Sugarek and narrated by Carin Gilfry. Chapter one, "Where is Emma?" The enchanted forest was deep and green and splashed with--

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:46.130
<v Ira Glass>Can I just say, wouldn't it be incredible if I just now played you the entire audiobook? You paid, like, $85 for those seats, right? Actually, I can't play you the whole audiobook because Carin gets exactly, like, two and a half sentences into this book. And she stumbles on a word, and she decides, oh wait, I'll just start again. I'll just start from the beginning.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:54.170
<v Ira Glass>So she gets up to get out of the closet and start the tape again, start the recording again. And she tries to get out of the closet.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:58.350
<v Ira Glass>[DOOR RATTLING]

00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.920
<v Carin Gilfry>Oh, [BLEEP].

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.790
<v Ira Glass>And she discovers that she's locked in.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:13.250
<v Carin Gilfry>[CHUCKLING]. Oh, my god. Seriously?

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:23.470
<v Ira Glass>And Carin told me at first she thought, like, this isn't going to be that hard, right? After all, she has the iPad. There's Wi-Fi. She could call the front desk on Skype, right? But there was a problem.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:31.670
<v Carin Gilfry>This Wi-Fi was, like, half of one bar, and it kept just cutting out. But I found the hotel number.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.690
<v Ira Glass>This of course is on the recording.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:38.630
<v Carin Gilfry>212-661-9600.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:39.510
<v Carin Gilfry>[IPAD ALERT NOISE]

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.550
<v Carin Gilfry>I dialed it.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.550
<v Carin Gilfry>[PHONE RINGING]

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:54.110
<v Recording>Thank you for calling the Roosevelt Hotel. Please listen carefully to the following options. For additional information about the hotel, please visit our website--

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:58.820
<v Carin Gilfry>And then, of course, it's, like, the longest hotel phone menu ever.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:09.280
<v Recording>--for sales and catering services, press 3. For accounting, press 4. For the human resources office, press 5.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:20.070
<v Carin Gilfry>I'm trying to press 0 to get to the operator. And I'm thinking the Wi-Fi is going to cut out at any moment. So I'm listening to this giant long menu, and finally I hear--

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.350
<v Recording>If at any time, you wish to speak to an operator, press 8.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:24.320
<v Recording>[PHONE BUTTON]

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.250
<v Carin Gilfry>So I press 8.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.400
<v Carin Gilfry>[PHONE RINGING]

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.870
<v Front Desk>Reservations. May speaking. May I assist you?

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.050
<v Carin Gilfry>Hi, I'm actually in room 1136.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:33.840
<v Front Desk>Hello?

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.500
<v Carin Gilfry>Can you hear me? Can you hear me?

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.060
<v Carin Gilfry>[CLICK]

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:44.590
<v Carin Gilfry>[BLEEP]

00:03:44.590 --> 00:04:04.800
<v Ira Glass>After that, there's seven minutes of silence on the recording-- just, like, total silence. Nothing happens except every now and then, Carin just laughs to herself. It happens one minute and 48 seconds after the phone call. Then it's quiet. There's no other sound for 44 seconds. And then on the recording, you hear--

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.840
<v Carin Gilfry>[CHUCKLING]

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:37.680
<v Ira Glass>Now and then, she tries the door handle again, thinking, she told me later, this can't really be true. Like, this can't-- there's got to be a way this is going to work. One wall of the closet has the hallway on the other side. And 18 minutes after Carin locks herself in, she hears people. She hears some German tourists walking towards her down the hall. And fortunately, she actually has the skills for this very situation.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:54.970
<v Carin Gilfry>[SPEAKING GERMAN] Can someone help me?

00:04:54.970 --> 00:05:04.420
<v Ira Glass>The Germans come to the door of the room. They whisper to each other for a little bit. And then, they do nothing.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:10.840
<v Carin Gilfry>Hello?

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:23.750
<v Ira Glass>Her husband is out on an audition-- he's also a singer-- and his phone was turned off. She texts her mom, hears nothing back. She phones her mom. And right then, somebody taps on the door of the hotel room and calls through it. "Are you OK?"

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:26.030
<v Carin Gilfry>Yes! Please, come in!

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:29.470
<v Ira Glass>"Can you come to the door?" the person asks.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:35.180
<v Carin Gilfry>I can't. I'm-- I'm stuck in the closet. [CHUCKLING]

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.080
<v Housekeeper>Hello? Where are you?

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:43.210
<v Carin Gilfry>In the closet right here. Oh, thank you. [LAUGHTER] I'm an audiobook narrator--

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:43.710
<v Housekeeper>Oh, OK.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.390
<v Carin Gilfry>--and I was  trying to narrate a book.

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:47.810
<v Houskeeper>Good. Oh--

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:48.780
<v Carin Gilfry>Oh, my god.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:06:05.690
<v Ira Glass>Carin knows that's a really weird thing to say to somebody as you're getting rescued, but she just couldn't help herself. Nobody wants to look like a nut, you know? Apparently the Germans went and they found this very sweet Jamaican housekeeper. And 22 minutes after Carin was locked in, the housekeeper set her free.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:13.480
<v Carin Gilfry>God, I was so happy to see her. And she was so nice. Oh, my gosh, she was so nice.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:20.340
<v Ira Glass>I know this is a weird question, but is it appropriate to tip in a situation like that?

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:23.170
<v Carin Gilfry>I don't know. I didn't think of it then.

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:47.810
<v Ira Glass>Now Carin, remember, she's an opera singer. And operas are all about spectacle. It's 80 people on the stage and horses and love and vengeance and big, grand feelings. It would really be hard to get further from that to what happened to her in that closet, right, where all the action takes place in a space not much bigger than your body. It is the simplest plot imaginable. There's literally no movement in this plot.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:56.310
<v Ira Glass>If someone were to stage this as an opera, how would that go?

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:59.520
<v Carin Gilfry>It might be minimalist music, actually.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.060
<v Ira Glass>Oh, right.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:11.950
<v Carin Gilfry>Like, just a repeating theme over and over and over again with me yelling, help!

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:23.200
<v Ira Glass>And you know, people say those kinds of things in interviews, and then I'll put that quote at the end of the story. And it makes for a nice  ending-- put a little plinky music under it. Maybe you've heard our show.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:35.360
<v Ira Glass>But when we did this interview three or four weeks ago, I realized, oh, wait a second. For once in my life, I don't have to let this story stop here. I am actually going to be in an opera house very soon. I can reach higher with this.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:52.250
<v Ira Glass>I can take this to the next logical step-- the step it never gets to, the step you need an opera house for. And it turns out, I actually have a hook-up for the kind of music that Carin is talking about. Some of you know this. I have a cousin. His name is Philip Glass. He has--

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:57.420
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:16.010
<v Ira Glass>--written a number of minimalist operas. They have been performed here on this very stage since the 1980s-- Einstein on the Beach just this past fall. And so I called Philip, and he sat down to write. And so today, right now, I am pleased to present here on the Brooklyn Academy of Music Opera House stage--

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:19.160
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:33.840
<v Ira Glass>--the world premiere of his latest opera. I am not joking-- this opera is called Help . Commissioned for our program today on the BAM Opera House stage, his work is played by orchestras all over the world, please welcome Philip Glass.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:54.790
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:08:54.790 --> 00:09:04.520
<v Ira Glass>And to perform this with him, Jonathan Dinklage on violin, Emily Brausa on cello, and, of course, the woman this is all about, Carin Gilfry, mezzo-soprano. Carin?

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:26.390
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:09:26.390 --> 00:10:08.140
<v Carin Gilfry>(SINGING) Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help! Help!

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:12.720
<v Carin Gilfry>[APPLAUSE]

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:19.690
<v Ira Glass>Carin Gilfry, with Jonathan Dinklage, Emily Brausa, and Philip Glass.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:29.570
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:49.610
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Ladies and gentlemen, for decades, the Brooklyn Academy of Music has been a home for all sorts of new kinds of theater and dance and music and opera. And when they invited our program to perform here today, we wanted to live up to that tradition by trying to invent something new.

00:10:49.610 --> 00:11:08.860
<v Ira Glass>And so what you're in for today is true stories. It's journalism, like we always do, but presented as radio drama. And we think these are radio dramas like you have never heard before. We have journalism turned into opera, into a full-on Broadway musical. We have journalism turned into a play that is structured like a radio documentary.

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:22.140
<v Ira Glass>The fact is, there are so many ways you can tell a true story. There are so, so many ways. And today, instead of just applying the tools of journalism to everyday stories like we usually do, we try to harness the full power of music and theater.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:44.090
<v Ira Glass>Like Carin's story-- we are not done with Carin's story. Sure, you could do it is as a minimalist opera. But what if you wanted to stage it as kind of an old-school opera-opera, the kind that they take you on field trips to when you're a kid. Like, what would that be? I asked Carin in our interview, and she was game to speculate.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:59.330
<v Carin Gilfry>Oh, I don't know. I mean, as a whole opera? I mean, opera is long. Maybe the elves from the book would come and prance around and sing about the enchanted forest. That was the book I was narrating.

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:01.290
<v Carin Gilfry>[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:11.740
<v Opera Chorus>(SINGING) We are the elves from Carin's story. We are the elves from Carin's story. We are the elves from Carin's story. We are the elves from Carin's story. We are the elves from Carin's story. We are the elves from Carin's story.

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:17.780
<v Opera Chorus>We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves.

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:23.720
<v Opera Chorus>My name is Cheets. I'm Violet . My name is Cheets. I'm Violet.

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:36.590
<v Opera Chorus>We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves. We are the elves!

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:43.020
<v Opera Chorus>[APPLAUSE]

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:47.370
<v Ira Glass>Of course, you would need more than elves in this story. Like, also you would want--

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:56.960
<v Carin Gilfry>The people. The people walking around the closet. The German people. There could be, you know, a "just got to New York" aria sung in German.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:57.910
<v Carin Gilfry>[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:04.810
<v German Couple>[SINGING IN GERMAN]

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:10.920
<v Ira Glass>Actually what they're singing is, we have just arrived. The air is dirty and smoggy.

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:40.860
<v German Couple>[SINGING IN GERMAN]

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:47.720
<v German Couple>[APPLAUSE]

00:13:47.720 --> 00:14:02.900
<v Ira Glass>I don't know if you caught the German there. One of the tourists says, I think someone called for help in German. And the other one says, impossible-- everyone in this country is illiterate.

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:06.690
<v Ira Glass>Then there's the front desk lady-- you know, the one that Carin calls from her iPad.

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:22.080
<v Carin Gilfry>Right. It could be funny if she was like, oh, my life is so boring. I just answer this phone all day, and people ask for room service. You do a cutaway. I'm stuck in the closet. And finally, this is her big moment, and she doesn't even know it.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:23.880
<v Carin Gilfry>[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:41.400
<v Front Desk>(SINGING) Oh! It's so boring. It's boring as can be! It's boring to sit behind this desk. It's boring to be me!

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:43.870
<v Carin Gilfry>(SINGING) Hello? Are you there? Can you hear me?

00:14:43.870 --> 00:15:05.850
<v Front Desk>(SINGING) No one ever notices my radiant complexion. No one ever asks me for anything but directions.

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:11.680
<v Front Desk>[APPLAUSE]

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:23.080
<v Ira Glass>Then there's the husband. In this version of the story, we could actually see him out on his audition, belting his heart out, oblivious to the pain that his wife is going through.

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:27.300
<v Carin Gilfry>So maybe we could incorporate some kind of famous baritone aria.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:32.180
<v Ira Glass>I wonder, like, what would be the most appropriate and ironic aria for him to be singing.

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:38.720
<v Carin Gilfry>Oh, I don't know. Something about love, I'm sure. Or something about being a rescuer.

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:56.850
<v Ira Glass>We actually talked about what would be best. And we finally settled on a scene from Rigoletto , where the title character who loves his daughter more than anything in the world rages against courtiers who have locked her up in a tiny room. Open up that door, he sings. Open up.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:57.290
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:23.390
<v Husband>[SINGING ARIA FROM "RIGOLETTO"]

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:33.070
<v Husband>[APPLAUSE]

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:40.900
<v Husband>God, that was great. Carin will be so proud of me.

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:44.320
<v Ira Glass>Of course, the climax of our opera arrives with the entrance of the housekeeper.

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:49.200
<v Ira Glass>What should she be like in the opera version of this story?

00:16:49.200 --> 00:17:01.110
<v Carin Gilry>Um, gosh. I mean, maybe she just rescues people all day. Maybe somebody's having a baby, and she delivers a baby. And then she cleans up after it.

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:04.450
<v Ira Glass>Like, she saves a guy who's out on the ledge. She gets him to come inside.

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:10.800
<v Carin Gilfry>Yeah.

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:12.760
<v Carin Gilfry>[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:17:12.760 --> 00:18:03.440
<v Houskeeper>(SINGING) What is it now? You've locked yourself in? I just delivered a baby in room 310, rescued a hostage, extinguished a fire! It's time to retire. But first, this little door's no match for me! One little push, I'll set you free!

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:17.620
<v Houskeeper>[APPLAUSE]

00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:19.570
<v Houskeeper>[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:21.950
<v Carin Gilfry>(SINGING) I can't believe--

00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:23.790
<v Opera Chorus>(SINGING) She can't believe--

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:25.780
<v Carin Gilfry>(SINGING) I am free.

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:28.270
<v Opera Chorus>(SINGING) She is free.

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:30.260
<v Carin Gilfry>(SINGING) Out of the closet.

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:31.260
<v Opera Chorus>(SINGING) Out.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:34.250
<v Carin Gilfry>(SINGING) And into the light.

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:36.240
<v Opera Chorus>(SINGING) Her clothes are all the same.

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:41.720
<v Houskeeper>(SINGING) I don't get paid enough. I don't get paid enough.

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:44.500
<v Husband>(SINGING) Ah--

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:48.760
<v Husband>[GERMAN CONVERSATION]

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:55.520
<v Husband>[OPERA COUNTERPOINT]

00:18:55.520 --> 00:19:01.770
<v Husband>[GERMAN CONVERSATION]

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:09.770
<v Husband>[OPERA COUNTERPOINT]

00:19:09.770 --> 00:19:13.700
<v Carin Gilfry>Seriously. True story.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:17.150
<v Opera Cast>(SINGING) Ah!

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:29.010
<v Opera Cast>[APPLAUSE]

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:54.360
<v Ira Glass>Our opera, En Schrank Gefangen, or Trapped in the Closet , was composed by Matt Aucoin, music direction and keyboards by William Hobbs. It featured Rachel Feldsetin, Candice Hoyes and Sarah Craft as elves, Marnie Breckenridge as the front desk, Adrian Rosas as the husband, Jeanine de Bique as the housekeeper, Rod Gilfry, who's actually Karen's dad, as the German tourist with Heather Buck, and mezzo-soprano Carin Gilfry as herself.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:20:00.800
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:23.390
<v Ira Glass>Which brings us to act one. Act One, "21 Chump Street, the Musical." I've always wondered what it would mean to try one of our radio stories as a musical. I was taken to so many musicals as a kid. And I think because of that, my basic sense of what makes a satisfying story actually comes from musicals much more than from TV or movies.

00:20:23.390 --> 00:20:37.790
<v Ira Glass>If you think about, like, the classic old musicals-- Fiddler on the Roof or even A Chorus Line -- it's like, they're funny at the beginning. And then there's something really emotional. And they're about some bigger idea. They take you into this world. I just love that.

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:51.290
<v Ira Glass>And so what you're about to hear is our first attempt at a musical based on journalism. Probably 70% to 80% of what you're about to hear is verbatim quotes from interviews. The rest is artistic invention. I'll say that upfront.

00:20:51.290 --> 00:21:08.180
<v Ira Glass>It's based on a true story you may remember from our radio program. Back in May, 2011, at a bunch of high schools in Palm Beach County, Florida, a group of young police officers were sent undercover to pose as students. They went to classes. They ate in the cafeteria. They had fake Facebook accounts, of course.

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:29.150
<v Ira Glass>There had been complaints about drugs being sold at these schools. That's what they were there to deal with. And what happened next in this one school was transformed into a musical by a team of people who usually do this on Broadway. Pretty much everybody involved in this, the cast, the chorus, the musicians, the director, Michael Mayer-- all from Broadway. Words and music are by Lin-Manuel Miranda, probably best known--

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:33.600
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:42.050
<v Ira Glass>--probably best known for writing and starring in In the Heights . He will be your narrator.

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:43.450
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:52.020
<v Lin-Manuel Miranda>The plan was called Operation D-minus. And one of the schools included in the plan was Park Vista Community High School, where a kid named Justin Laboy--

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:53.490
<v Justin>(SINGING) That's me.

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:55.630
<v Lin-Manuel Miranda>--an 18-year-old honor roll student--

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:56.970
<v Justin>(SINGING) I get straight A's, man.

00:21:56.970 --> 00:22:03.820
<v Lin-Manuel Miranda>--was in the last semester of his senior year. Justin could hardly believe his luck, when a very pretty girl showed up.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:06.090
<v Justin>(SINGING) Naomi!

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:08.990
<v Lin-Manuel Miranda>In not one but two of his classes.

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:10.810
<v Students>(SINGING) Naomi!

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:15.090
<v Lin-Manuel Miranda>She sat in front of him. He switched seats.

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:17.940
<v Students>(SINGING) Naomi!

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:19.560
<v Lin-Manuel Miranda>The last name she used was--

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:23.760
<v Students>(SINGING) Rodriguez.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.890
<v Lin-Manuel Miranda>Justin, what drew you to Naomi initially?

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:37.280
<v Justin>(SINGING) Man, she used to fall asleep in class. She was a (SINGING) light-skinneded Puerto Rican-Dominican-- long hair, mature in the body like whoa.

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:38.180
<v Students>(SINGING) Like, whoa.

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:45.310
<v Justin>(SINGING) That's not the only reason I liked her, though. She said she moved with her mother to Florida from New York.

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:47.450
<v Students>(SINGING) Where dreams are made.

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:55.320
<v Justin>(SINGING) Well, so did I. So I said, hi. She seemed mature, and I talked more.

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:56.560
