00:40:22.310 --> 00:40:35.520
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so I bring him in the bedroom. But I'm so tired from the drive that I fall asleep, which is the only thing you cannot do when acclimating an indoor cat to a house. And so I wake up an hour later. Ivan is gone. He got out.

00:40:35.520 --> 00:41:03.800
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so now I'm running around the house. I'm like, my cat's going to explode. I wake up my wife. I was like, Clo-- her name's Jen-- I say, Mr. Fantastic is gone-- his name's Ivan. And Clo gave me a look that I can only describe as "divorce eyes."

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:16.970
<v Mike Birbiglia>Because before that, point I was convinced that we would be married forever. And then once I saw the divorce eyes, I was like, oh, I guess this could end. And if it ended, it would look a lot like that.

00:41:16.970 --> 00:41:37.940
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so now the two of us are running around the house. I'm like, my marriage is falling apart. My cat's going to explode. And we find Ivan, but we had another major problem in the house, which is that there were mice in the house.

00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:53.090
<v Mike Birbiglia>It was actually worse than that, because they were parasitic mice. They have what's called toxoplasmosis, which means they have a-- yeah, you might know what this is. They have a parasite in them, and as a result they're unafraid of cats, and they're unafraid of people.

00:41:53.090 --> 00:42:11.550
<v Mike Birbiglia>And the way we discovered this was that my wife was watching TV, and she looked next to her. And there was a mouse. And he was watching TV also. And she screamed!

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:29.010
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he just looked up at her like Stuart Little-- like, hey, what's going on? I don't like this show either. I don't know why all those women would want to marry that one guy.

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:50.390
<v Mike Birbiglia>And then she pushed him off the couch. And he didn't even run away. He didn't even scurry, which is a verb invented for mice. He just walked into the kitchen, like a roommate-- like, fine! I'll go in the other room! I just think you're overreacting.

00:42:50.390 --> 00:43:06.500
<v Mike Birbiglia>And then he did a confessional into the Mouse Cam in the kitchen. He was like, I'm not here to make friends. I'm here to win. I was here before they came. I'll be here when they're gone. I'm a mouse.

00:43:06.500 --> 00:43:14.420
<v Mike Birbiglia>[APPLAUSE]

00:43:14.420 --> 00:43:41.590
<v Ira Glass>That's from "Real Mouse-wives of Cats-achusetts." That night, I'm sound asleep, and my wife wakes me up by grabbing my face. She says, Mo-- my name's Mike-- Mr. Fantastic found the mouse. You need to get the mouse.

00:43:41.590 --> 00:44:12.950
<v Ira Glass>And I sit up, and I say Clo, we have a cat. We do everything for the cat. We gave him food. We give him an apartment that he thinks is the world. We set aside an area in the apartment for him to poop in that we clean more often than the area where we poop.

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:38.550
<v Ira Glass>We have a gentleman's agreement that in the unlikely event that a mouse should walk in that door (A LA MARLON BRANDO) that he will kill that mouse and we will never speak of that mouse again. And he will be protected. (NORMAL VOICE) That's from "Cat-father."

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:47.330
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:44:47.330 --> 00:45:07.380
<v Ira Glass>My wife says, Mo, get the mouse. And I sit up, and I see what may be the strangest tableau I will ever witness in my entire life. Ivan's smacking the mouse. The mouse flies in the air, lands, gets up, walks back towards Ivan.

00:45:07.380 --> 00:45:26.900
<v Ira Glass>Ivan smacks the mouse, flies in the air, lands, gets up, walks back towards Ivan. Ivan is thrilled! His toy is alive!

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:40.280
<v Ira Glass>I have a serious sleepwalking disorder. So as I am watching this, I'm not even really sure it's happening. I'm thinking, I've had this dream before.

00:45:40.280 --> 00:45:50.190
<v Ira Glass>My wife says, Mo, get the mouse. And she hands me a cup.

00:45:50.190 --> 00:46:25.100
<v Ira Glass>I sit up. I walk towards the mouse. And the mouse walks towards me. I put the cup over the mouse. I put a magazine under the cup. I take the cup into the backyard, and I put the mouse into the forest, where I can only assume that he walked into the mouth of a wolf.

00:46:25.100 --> 00:46:29.550
<v Ira Glass>And from that day forward, we have called it Mouse-achusetts.

00:46:29.550 --> 00:46:40.850
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:46:40.850 --> 00:47:04.480
<v Ira Glass>I want to point out something really special that happened there at the end. A few minutes ago, I prefaced this story with a Massachusetts-based pun, Cats-achusetts, which we all agreed as a group is not funny.

00:47:04.480 --> 00:47:27.550
<v Ira Glass>Just moments ago, I concluded the story with another Massachusetts-based pun that was nearly identical. And that was Mouse-achusetts. And we applauded.

00:47:27.550 --> 00:47:34.360
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:47:34.360 --> 00:47:40.940
<v Ira Glass>Which means, in a way, it's like we're married.

00:47:40.940 --> 00:47:48.310
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

00:47:48.310 --> 00:48:09.710
<v Ira Glass>Mike Birbiglia. He's on his hundred-city "Thank God for Jokes" tour, which, Chicagolandians, comes to the Chicago Theater in September. To everybody hearing us on the radio, there was a person in a mouse costume on roller skates in that story doing some funny stuff. To see the video of this entire show so you understand what people are laughing at-- you will not be sorry-- thisamericanlife.org.

00:48:09.710 --> 00:48:16.350
<v Ira Glass>We have arrived at act three of our show. Act Three, "How do you Slow this Thing Down?"

00:48:16.350 --> 00:48:35.110
<v Ira Glass>So this story is a follow up to a story that we ran a couple of years ago, which was a full episode of our show about one of our contributors, Josh Bearman, and his family. The set-up is that Josh's parents got divorced when he was very young. And he and his brother Ethan grew up with their dad, who's a physicist, out in suburban Los Angeles.

00:48:35.110 --> 00:48:50.630
<v Ira Glass>Josh's mom and his half-brother David lived very differently. They drifted around. They ended up in Florida. They were barely keeping it together. Josh's mom was an alcoholic. David was an aspiring rapper getting acquainted with the court system

00:48:50.630 --> 00:49:06.930
<v Ira Glass>Together they lived in this tiny condo in a retirement community in West Palm Beach called Century Village, where they did not belong. They were not retirees. And they would get into various kinds of jams and crises. And Josh would have to fly to Florida to try to help. He ended up spending a lot of time there.

00:49:06.930 --> 00:49:33.110
<v Ira Glass>What you are about to hear is an experiment at creating a radio drama which has exactly the same structure as one of the documentary stories on our show. And so what's going to happen is the real Josh Bearman is going to narrate. And then instead of going to, like, quotes on tape or things like that, we have actors performing scenes that really happened. All of this really happened. In those scenes, Josh is played by Josh Hamilton. His brother David is played by James Ransone.

00:49:33.110 --> 00:49:41.950
<v Ira Glass>The story begins last year when real-life Josh found himself back in Florida with his two brothers.

00:49:41.950 --> 00:50:02.030
<v Josh Bearman>My mother's been in the hospital so much by now she's figured out how to direct the paramedics, like emergency chauffeurs or something, to all of the best facilities. Her room at Wellington Regional is big and bright. It's a corner room with big windows and a nice view. Although she can't see it, because right now she's connected to state-of-the-art equipment behind a curtain.

00:50:02.030 --> 00:50:02.640
<v Josh Bearman>[SOUNDS OF MEDICAL EQUIPMENT]

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:06.760
<v Josh Bearman>When I walk in, my brother David is there already.

00:50:06.760 --> 00:50:13.920
<v David>Hey, man. Oh, this whole thing was like a fucked-up remix of "The Night Before Christmas," because it was the night before Christmas.

00:50:13.920 --> 00:50:15.690
<v Josh>Is this story gonna be in rhyme, or--

00:50:15.690 --> 00:50:17.170
<v David>Shut up.

00:50:17.170 --> 00:50:18.960
<v Josh>David, what happened?

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:23.830
<v David>You know how she gets around the holidays. I mean, I thought that you and Ethan were going to bring her out to California.

00:50:23.830 --> 00:50:29.800
<v Josh>Oh, man. I don't know why we didn't. I think it was Ethan's schedule. Or we probably just didn't try hard enough.

00:50:29.800 --> 00:50:36.580
<v David>Nah, nah. I mean, you never know when she's going to get all twisted like that. And I wasn't even really around anyway.

00:50:36.580 --> 00:50:38.040
<v Josh>Where were you?

00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:47.480
<v David>I was with this girl from school, this girl Tasha. And I went over to her house early on Christmas Eve, because they were having turkey dinner and everything-- you know, like a normal family.

00:50:47.480 --> 00:50:58.170
<v David>I mean, I had to get out of Century Village. I was cooped up in there with Mom, and she was driving me crazy. So anyway, I'm at Tasha's. And Mom calls, and she sounds all drunk, right? And she's like, something happened to the car.

00:50:58.170 --> 00:50:59.980
<v Josh>Something hap-- what kind of something?

00:50:59.980 --> 00:51:02.060
<v David>I don't know! That's what I said!

00:51:02.060 --> 00:51:03.890
<v Josh>That's like something you would tell me.

00:51:03.890 --> 00:51:12.930
<v David>I know. And now I know how you feel. Well, whatever happened, the car got stuck, right? And I didn't have any way to get to her.

00:51:12.930 --> 00:51:14.720
<v Josh>So how did she get home?

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:28.250
<v David>I think Peggy picked her up. And by the time I got back to the condo the next day, she was-- she was pretty deep in it. But it didn't seem like that big of a deal really. And then she woke up, and she was short of breath, so I was like--

00:51:28.250 --> 00:51:31.100
<v Josh>Wait, wait, wait. For how long?

00:51:31.100 --> 00:51:33.720
<v David>Tuesday, so for, like, a day.

00:51:33.720 --> 00:51:35.310
<v Josh>And you didn't call me sooner?

00:51:35.310 --> 00:51:44.210
<v David>Dude, she's done this shit, like, a million times. I mean, she wasn't even that worried about it. And she didn't want me to ride in the ambulance with her. She was like, I'll see you later tonight, Dave.

00:51:44.210 --> 00:51:50.050
<v Dr. Snyder>Hi, I'm Dr. Snyder. Are-- are you the older brother?

00:51:50.050 --> 00:51:51.020
<v Josh>Yes.

00:51:51.020 --> 00:51:52.670
<v Dr. Snyder>You have the medical power of attorney?

00:51:52.670 --> 00:51:53.120
<v Josh>Yes.

00:51:53.120 --> 00:51:56.660
<v Dr. Snyder>Good. And who's Ethan?

00:51:56.660 --> 00:51:58.150
<v Josh>Our other brother. He's also coming from California.

00:51:58.150 --> 00:51:59.890
<v Dr. Snyder>He has the medical power of attorney?

00:51:59.890 --> 00:52:01.730
<v Josh>Yes.

00:52:01.730 --> 00:52:03.280
<v Dr. Snyder>Not him?

00:52:03.280 --> 00:52:04.990
<v Josh>No.

00:52:04.990 --> 00:52:10.780
<v Dr. Snyder>OK. Oh, uh, did your mother go to Cornell?

00:52:10.780 --> 00:52:12.320
<v Josh>Yes, why?

00:52:12.320 --> 00:52:13.600
<v Dr. Snyder>She told us.

00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:14.900
<v Josh>What else did she say?

00:52:14.900 --> 00:52:19.390
<v Dr. Snyder>That she does not consume alcohol.

00:52:19.390 --> 00:52:40.400
<v Josh Bearman>Those were her last words before going under. Soon enough, withdrawal told a very different story. The first time this happened was five years ago, when David called and told me he had to remit himself to the local sheriff's office for, quote, "just a quick jail sentence." Oh, and Mom's in the hospital.

00:52:40.400 --> 00:52:43.170
<v Josh Bearman>So I flew to Florida that next day and went straight to the ICU.

00:52:43.170 --> 00:52:43.770
<v Josh Bearman>[SOUND OF AIRPLANE FLYING OVERHEAD]

00:52:43.770 --> 00:52:46.600
<v Josh Bearman>And here's what happened that time.

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:50.580
<v Orderly>Well, looks like she'll never be the same again.

00:52:50.580 --> 00:52:52.890
<v Josh>What, are you the neurologist?

00:52:52.890 --> 00:53:08.020
<v Orderly>Oh, I see we have a savvy medical consumer here. No. It's 2:00 AM. I'm just the guy on rotation. So she scored low on the GOAT test, which measures brain function. She got a 62-- probably permanently impaired.

00:53:08.020 --> 00:53:10.470
<v Josh>62? Out of what?

00:53:10.470 --> 00:53:11.860
<v Orderly>Out of 100.

00:53:11.860 --> 00:53:14.880
<v Nurse 1>Although no one ever really scores 100.

00:53:14.880 --> 00:53:19.150
<v Nurse 2>Oh, that's true. I took it myself for practice in nursing school. I got, like, an 80.

00:53:19.150 --> 00:53:22.180
<v Nurse 1>Yeah, for a perfect score, you'd have to be, like, Superman or something.

00:53:22.180 --> 00:53:30.020
<v Orderly>No, no, no. You know who could get 100? What's that guy-- you know, that guy who can do anything including karate.

00:53:30.020 --> 00:53:33.770
<v Nurse 1>Leonardo da Vinci.

00:53:33.770 --> 00:53:39.640
<v Orderly>No, no, no. He's dead. He would score poorly. [CHUCKLING] I was just--

00:53:39.640 --> 00:53:55.200
<v Orderly>No, the-- the speaker guy who can do anything! Jesus Christ, if I was him, I'd never forget anyone's name. Uh-- he helps you realize your full potential. He's six and a half feet tall. Come on, ladies. He's, uh--

00:53:55.200 --> 00:53:56.500
<v Josh>Tony Robbins?

00:53:56.500 --> 00:54:07.870
<v Orderly>Yes. Exactly. That guy. That guy, he could score 100.

00:54:07.870 --> 00:54:33.150
<v Josh Bearman>So as the medical professionals discussed how awakening the giant within can totally boost your neurological scores, one of the other nurses came and told me that earlier they had had to restrain my mom because even though she was unconscious, she'd somehow managed to get a hold of cigarettes. They caught her trying to light one in bed. We're not even sure where she got it, the nurse said.

00:54:33.150 --> 00:54:44.670
<v Josh Bearman>And that's when I knew my mother would be fine. Within days, she was sitting up, completely lucid, chatting up strangers-- everyone's favorite.

00:54:44.670 --> 00:55:00.560
<v Josh Bearman>This time, things were bad enough that I called my brother Ethan. He can only come in a true emergency because he plays for the LA Philharmonic, and he has a couple kids, and his schedule's planned out months in advance. He took the red-eye straight from playing at Disney Hall.

00:55:00.560 --> 00:55:02.540
<v Ethan>Well, here we are again.

00:55:02.540 --> 00:55:03.580
<v Josh>How you doing, man?

00:55:03.580 --> 00:55:04.800
<v Ethan>I'm so glad you're here.

00:55:04.800 --> 00:55:05.730
<v Josh>Thanks for coming.

00:55:05.730 --> 00:55:06.180
<v Ethan>Hey, buddy.

00:55:06.180 --> 00:55:06.780
<v David>Good to see you.

00:55:06.780 --> 00:55:07.410
<v Ethan>You holding up?

00:55:07.410 --> 00:55:09.510
<v David>Yeah, I'm all right. Nice tux.

00:55:09.510 --> 00:55:15.730
<v Ethan>Yeah. Thought my stage attire might help me look a little more official. She in there?

00:55:15.730 --> 00:55:24.300
<v Josh Bearman>Right now Ethan is working on a giant Mahler retrospective. They're going to play all nine symphonies. It's something that's never been done before.

00:55:24.300 --> 00:55:27.210
<v Josh>Oh, Ethan, this is Dr. Snyder.

00:55:27.210 --> 00:55:42.910
<v Dr. Snyder>Hi. It's nice to meet you. Your mother had a heart attack. She has severe respiratory distress, nearly septic infection. If we can get her  breathing on her own again, recovery is possible but would take a long time.

00:55:42.910 --> 00:55:46.160
<v Ethan>And then what?

00:55:46.160 --> 00:55:50.600
<v Dr. Snyder>That's a good question.

00:55:50.600 --> 00:56:00.500
<v Josh>No, that is a good question. If she needs in-home care, I don't think David could do it. They can't keep the cable on. And if she doesn't make it, what then?

00:56:00.500 --> 00:56:01.400
<v Ethan>Where is he going to live?

00:56:01.400 --> 00:56:18.430
<v Josh>Right? He can't stay at Century Village. I mean, it's a retirement community with a perimeter. It's like a geriatric army base. I don't know how he hasn't gotten kicked out already. They're definitely onto him. And if they realize mom isn't coming back--

00:56:18.430 --> 00:56:25.080
<v Ethan>I'm sure we're going to get calls any day now.

00:56:25.080 --> 00:56:26.850
<v Josh>Which one are you working on?

00:56:26.850 --> 00:56:28.500
<v Ethan>Oh. The 8th.

00:56:28.500 --> 00:56:30.710
<v Josh>Oh, that's a big one, right?

00:56:30.710 --> 00:56:34.920
<v Ethan>Yeah, it's a big one. It has some really nice passages, though.

00:56:34.920 --> 00:56:44.890
<v David>Mom, I know you can hear me. We love you, and we need you.

00:56:44.890 --> 00:56:47.670
<v Josh>What's it about?

00:56:47.670 --> 00:56:52.560
<v Ethan>Eternal life.

00:56:52.560 --> 00:56:59.840
<v Ethan>[MUSIC - MAHLER'S  "SYMPHONY NUMBER 8"]

00:56:59.840 --> 00:57:30.300
<v Josh Bearman>It's David who says that Mom would be happy that all three of her boys were together. And that's true. One of the few real senses of family I can recall was summers in the Midwest with her when we were all children years ago. And we made ice cream and caught fireflies and roller skated-- normal things like that. That's why David doesn't like being in the room much now, because he doesn't want to remember Mom like this, trapped in so many tubes. It's like some jacked-up Darth Vader shit, he says.

00:57:30.300 --> 00:57:42.700
<v Josh Bearman>And yet, he's the one who's been at her bedside the most. I can't bring myself to kiss her the way David does. I don't know why.

00:57:42.700 --> 00:58:05.960
<v Josh Bearman>After Ethan goes back to California, I stay with David and Mom. Time is merciless in Florida and especially merciless in the ICU. Days turn into weeks, and every afternoon I pick up David, and we go to the hospital, talk to some doctors, wait, talk to some more doctors, and then maybe get dinner. Very little changes.

00:58:05.960 --> 00:58:09.310
<v David>Yo! Yo, I still want to show you that video I was telling you about.

00:58:09.310 --> 00:58:10.900
<v Josh>Oh. Yeah, right. OK.

00:58:10.900 --> 00:58:12.530
<v David>OK,  hold up. Hold up.

00:58:12.530 --> 00:58:27.410
<v Josh Bearman>In this very strange video, David's friend Sebastian, a completely grown man, is standing on some Florida crabgrass breakdancing robot-style. And the music is a tinny Casio-sounding version of "Fur Elise."

00:58:27.410 --> 00:58:34.760
<v Josh Bearman>[MUSIC  - "FUR ELISE"]

00:58:34.760 --> 00:58:36.230
<v Josh>You know, he's pretty good.

00:58:36.230 --> 00:58:37.110
<v David>I know.

00:58:37.110 --> 00:58:40.210
<v Josh>But why is this happening?

00:58:40.210 --> 00:58:58.390
<v David>I don't even really know. I mean, we were all just hanging out there in the driveway-- like, a bunch of us. Dude just starts doing the robot. He does that sometimes, right? And this one time, he was doing it in my face for a really long time-- for, like, a half hour. No, no-- I couldn't even believe it, man.

00:58:58.390 --> 00:58:59.900
<v Josh>That he could keep it up that long?

00:58:59.900 --> 00:59:10.610
<v David>No, more that I had to watch it for so long. Because I was, like, kinda trapped there, right? And at first, I was trying not to look. And then I couldn't look away.

00:59:10.610 --> 00:59:11.750
<v Josh>Let me see that again.

00:59:11.750 --> 00:59:13.100
<v David>OK, all right. Yeah, yeah.

00:59:13.100 --> 00:59:13.690
<v David>[MUSIC - "FUR ELISE"]

00:59:13.690 --> 00:59:18.360
<v David>It was like I was in denial at first. And then I accepted it.

00:59:18.360 --> 00:59:19.750
<v Josh>Like the stages of grief.

00:59:19.750 --> 00:59:28.720
<v David>Exactly! Exactly. And then I reached some kind of state of bliss where it was so awesome that I was almost in tears.

00:59:28.720 --> 00:59:31.940
<v Josh>Yeah, I'm not sure that's exactly the Kubler-Ross model.

00:59:31.940 --> 00:59:36.940
<v David>Well, they should add that one-- the awesome state.

00:59:36.940 --> 00:59:46.080
<v Josh Bearman>It's nice spending time with David. But I'm also really worried about him. He's had a history with pills on and off over the years. And I suspect it's on again.

00:59:46.080 --> 01:00:03.460
<v Josh Bearman>One night while we're driving to dinner, David says, oh hey, Josh, can we stop over at the Victory? That's a gas station down on Okeechobee Boulevard. And Victory's a total misnomer for the kind of place where you pull in and just see a bunch of weird dudes crouching around the entrance, like it's a totally normal social spot.

01:00:03.460 --> 01:00:23.630
<v Josh Bearman>And David knows everyone here, which is weird. And I'm pretty sure something illicit is happening but I don't want to ask, because I don't want to be The Man with David. And he probably wouldn't tell me anyhow. Ah, it's nothing, David says. Sure, just your regular nighttime rendezvous in the parking lot of an off-brand gas station.

01:00:23.630 --> 01:00:42.370
<v Josh Bearman>David gets back in the car, and he says, all right, I'm good. But I'm not so sure. It's weird, but Mom and David are a team-- like a fucked-up crazy team for a long time, but a team nonetheless. And they've taken care of each other. And the question on my mind is if Mom doesn't make it whether David will.

01:00:42.370 --> 01:00:48.520
<v Dr. Snyder>All right, guys. She might destabilize immediately, but if she makes it, we'll load her up and get her over to the hospice facility right away.

01:00:48.520 --> 01:00:53.710
<v David>Yeah, I'm just-- I'm a little concerned that she understands you. I mean, I think that she can hear you.

01:00:53.710 --> 01:00:58.170
<v Dr. Snyder>Oh, I know this can be hard for you, an adjustment to end-of-life care is always--

01:00:58.170 --> 01:01:01.090
<v David>I mean, she's right there.

01:01:01.090 --> 01:01:02.950
<v Dr. Snyder>Right. Do you have any other questions?

01:01:02.950 --> 01:01:03.860
<v Josh>Well, I was wondering--

01:01:03.860 --> 01:01:10.840
<v David>Yeah. Yeah, yeah. Yeah. I was wondering about Xanax.

01:01:10.840 --> 01:01:16.030
<v Dr. Snyder>Uh, she's already getting Ativan, which is also for anxiety. It's part of the hospice program. So she--

01:01:16.030 --> 01:01:26.760
<v David>No, I meant for me. Look, I'm not talking, like, a whole prescription or anything. I'm just asking if you could hook me up with, like, a couple, you know, for right now.

01:01:26.760 --> 01:01:34.970
<v Dr. Snyder>I'm sorry. I can't accommodate that request.

01:01:34.970 --> 01:01:41.610
<v David>I'm gonna go outside and smoke. This shit is getting a little too real for me right now.

01:01:41.610 --> 01:01:52.540
<v Josh Bearman>The hospice is nice-- institutional, but warm. Like, people are allowed to bring pets and stuff. One lady even brought her horse, the intake guy was very excited to tell us.

01:01:52.540 --> 01:02:04.090
<v Josh Bearman>I spent some time going through Mom's things. I find her address book, which is a mess, stuffed with Post-Its and scraps, bearing faded numbers in Mom's perfect cursive.

01:02:04.090 --> 01:02:09.010
<v Josh>How can we call her friends? I don't know who's who. I mean, no one visited her in the hospital.

01:02:09.010 --> 01:02:10.580
<v Josh>[PHONE VIBRATING]

01:02:10.580 --> 01:02:12.400
<v Josh>We should have called people sooner.

01:02:12.400 --> 01:02:17.900
<v David>She doesn't really have that many friends around anymore. And I'm telling you, she didn't want visitors at the hospital.

01:02:17.900 --> 01:02:20.140
<v Josh>Yes, but they might want to see her.

01:02:20.140 --> 01:02:21.760
<v David>Well, I don't have her friends' numbers.

01:02:21.760 --> 01:02:24.950
<v Josh>David, I need you to help me figure this out.

01:02:24.950 --> 01:02:25.740
<v Josh>[PHONE VIBRATING]

01:02:25.740 --> 01:02:27.090
<v David>I gotta take care of some shit right now.

01:02:27.090 --> 01:02:33.700
<v Josh>Where are you going? Dude, you've been wandering in and out of here for weeks. Is it pills or what?

01:02:33.700 --> 01:02:37.960
<v Dr. Snyder>Nah. Nah, it's Sebastian, right? He's got this other friend who called him, and--

01:02:37.960 --> 01:02:51.540
<v Josh>So? That can wait. What is with all the weird petty dramas? Half the time you're pacing around on your phone dealing with some crisis, like Jimmy Carter at Camp David.

01:02:51.540 --> 01:02:53.380
<v David>Well, I got Peggy's number.

01:02:53.380 --> 01:02:55.090
<v Josh>Well, why didn't you tell me that before?

01:02:55.090 --> 01:02:56.050
<v David>You didn't ask?

01:02:56.050 --> 01:03:02.380
<v Josh>Dude! This is when you call people. When your mother is dying, you call her fucking friends.

01:03:02.380 --> 01:03:14.760
<v David>Yo, she can hear you. And I know that you want to be all mad at me right now because you're mad at yourself or something, but this whole situation is fucked up. I accepted it a long time ago.

01:03:14.760 --> 01:03:16.920
<v Josh>I accept that it's fucked up. I've been here for six weeks.

01:03:16.920 --> 01:03:40.370
<v David>And I appreciate everything that you have done. But I've been here the whole time-- when Mom fell and knocked herself out or when I found her in the backyard or when I took her to get the last of her teeth removed. I wish it was different, but it is what it is. Mom is my best friend.

01:03:40.370 --> 01:04:02.810
<v David>And you, you're gonna get to go home. And I'm going to be the one who's stranded here. Now, I have to go. Sebastian locked himself in his garage somehow. And you should call Peggy, but do that shit outside.

01:04:02.810 --> 01:04:11.200
<v Josh Bearman>I finally bring myself to start holding Mom's hands, and she rubs my palm. I think David's right. She knows.

01:04:11.200 --> 01:04:37.040
<v Josh Bearman>Each time, her grip gets a little stronger. I look in her terrified eyes and see my own. One night, just before I leave for the day, she takes my hand with both of hers. It's the biggest show of purpose in weeks. And for a second, I don't see the tubes, the room, the cracked lips, or her papery fingers. No one else is here. It's just us.

01:04:37.040 --> 01:04:53.640
<v Josh Bearman>The next morning, there was a message on my phone from the hospice before I got up. I know-- they always say it was peaceful. And they made arrangements with the King David Cemetery for the next day.

01:04:53.640 --> 01:05:02.820
<v David>Damn, my stomach hurts. I can't tell if it's this funeral stuff or from that bacon, egg, and cheese biscuit I ate this morning.

01:05:02.820 --> 01:05:06.750
<v Josh>Hm. I mean, I'm going to guess it's the former.

01:05:06.750 --> 01:05:22.750
<v David>I don't know. That shit was pretty gross. You want to hear something funny? So, I was at home earlier today, right, watching TV, just trying to take my mind off of things, you know?

01:05:22.750 --> 01:05:36.840
<v David>And then all of a sudden, the TV goes black. And I'm like, oh, shit. This is all I need right now. So I find this cable bill from before Mom went into the hospital. It was, like, $15, but she forgot to pay it, as usual.

01:05:36.840 --> 01:05:57.890
<v David>And the whole thing was, like, such a Mom-type scenario, you know? I mean, it's just exactly the type of thing that she would have done. So when the TV went black, I-- I kinda thought it was like Mom saying a little hello. I'll be OK.

01:05:57.890 --> 01:05:59.760
<v Josh>I hope so.

01:05:59.760 --> 01:06:02.970
<v Rabbi>All right. Let's get rolling.

01:06:02.970 --> 01:06:03.410
<v Rabbi>[RABBI CONDUCTING THE SERVICE IN THE BACKGROUND]

01:06:03.410 --> 01:06:18.060
<v Josh Bearman>The sun is low on the horizon beneath the clouds, bringing up steam from the grass. If you include the funeral director and the two Haitian dudes lounging on tombstones waiting to fill in the grave, there are only nine people listening.

01:06:18.060 --> 01:06:37.190
<v Josh Bearman>The scene feels like a frontier funeral, a clutch of poor souls standing around a pine box, like we should leave here in a covered wagon and keep heading west and just be glad the devil's drink didn't bury us, too.

01:06:37.190 --> 01:06:59.970
<v Josh Bearman>[APPLAUSE]

01:06:59.970 --> 01:07:18.580
<v Ira Glass>Joshuah Bearman. In the scenes, Josh Hamilton played him. James Ransone played his brother David. Matt Marks was Ethan and played the French horn. Our doctors were Bhavesh Patel and Seth Barrish, who also played the rabbi. Nurses were Carolyn Baeumler and Zakiya Young. Terry Kinney directed the story on the stage.

01:07:18.580 --> 01:07:44.050
<v Ira Glass>So we've been planning for months that Stephin Merritt was going to sing a song at this point in the show. And we told Josh Bearman a couple weeks ago. And he was like, oh my god, that's crazy. My mom-- his mom in the story-- used to babysit for Stephin Merritt. It's true-- when Stephin was a kid growing up outside Boston, Josh Bearman's mom Susan babysat for him. So please welcome, Stephin Merritt.

01:07:44.050 --> 01:07:59.020
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

01:07:59.020 --> 01:10:45.740
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "HOW DO YOU SLOW THIS THING DOWN?" BY STEPHIN MERRITT]

01:10:45.740 --> 01:10:57.710
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

01:10:57.710 --> 01:11:08.950
<v Ira Glass>Stephin Merritt, with Pinky Weitzman on violin and Sam Davol on cello. Stephin Merritt plays in a number of bands. His latest album is from The Magnetic Fields. It's called Love at the Bottom of the Sea .

01:11:08.950 --> 01:11:19.160
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

01:11:19.160 --> 01:11:40.490
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, "Bus, Stop!" This next story is something that comedian Sasheer Zamata has been telling onstage in her stand-up act. And we asked her to adapt it into a full-on radio drama with actors and a sound man, like a classic radio drama. In fact, we have two sound men doing sound effects with a table full of props. Please welcome Sasheer Zamata.

01:11:40.490 --> 01:11:54.260
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

01:11:54.260 --> 01:12:11.490
<v Sasheer Zamata>A few years ago, I got into an accident on a shuttle bus. My friend Nicole and I rented a car, and we were dropping it off at LaGuardia airport. We got onto the rental car company's shuttle bus to get taken to the cab stand to get a cab to go home. And we were the only two passengers on the bus.

01:12:11.490 --> 01:12:17.920
<v Bus Driver>Hey, you two sure you want to sit back there? Because, ha-ha, I drive pretty crazy.

01:12:17.920 --> 01:12:18.380
<v Bus Driver>[NERVOUS CHUCKLING]

01:12:18.380 --> 01:12:19.780
<v Nicole>OK!

01:12:19.780 --> 01:12:23.360
<v Sasheer>OK.

01:12:23.360 --> 01:12:32.600
<v Sasheer Zamata>We heard him say this, but we didn't believe him. We should have believed him. Because that's exactly what he did.

01:12:32.600 --> 01:12:33.770
<v Sasheer Zamata>[BRAKES RELEASING]

01:12:33.770 --> 01:12:42.880
<v Sasheer Zamata>He sped out of the parking lot and towards the airport. And Nicole and I were in the back, humoring him and playing up the wild ride.

01:12:42.880 --> 01:12:48.090
<v Nicole And Sasheer>Woo! This is crazy!

01:12:48.090 --> 01:12:50.480
<v Sasheer Zamata>And then he hits the brakes really hard.

01:12:50.480 --> 01:12:51.830
<v Sasheer Zamata>[BRAKES  SQUEALING]

01:12:51.830 --> 01:12:55.780
<v Nicole And Sasheer>Whoa! Too crazy!

01:12:55.780 --> 01:12:58.600
<v Sasheer Zamata>And then we hit another car in the intersection.

01:12:58.600 --> 01:12:59.920
<v Sasheer Zamata>[RUMBLING]

01:12:59.920 --> 01:13:03.630
<v Nicole And Sasheer>Way too crazy!

01:13:03.630 --> 01:13:13.750
<v Sasheer Zamata>There's no seat belts on the bus, and Nicole goes falling into the luggage carry in front of her. I go flying into the air and headed towards the driver's seat.

01:13:13.750 --> 01:13:25.590
<v Sasheer Zamata>Now, if I was walking this distance, it would probably take me, like, 10 seconds. But because I'm flying through the air, it takes me, like, two. And I think now I understand why flying is the fastest way to travel.

01:13:25.590 --> 01:13:26.480
<v Sasheer Zamata>[CLUNK]

01:13:26.480 --> 01:13:29.140
<v Sasheer>Ow!

01:13:29.140 --> 01:14:03.000
<v Sasheer Zamata>I'm lying on the floor, and my head is pounding. And I just think, wow, that was the most realistic example of foreshadowing I've ever seen. Two seconds ago, this guy says, I drive crazy. And then he did. And then we got into an accident. I don't know why I thought anything different would happen.

01:14:03.000 --> 01:14:04.410
<v Bus Driver>Hey, you OK?

01:14:04.410 --> 01:14:06.530
<v Sasheer>No. Everything sucks. I want to go home.

01:14:06.530 --> 01:14:08.500
<v Nicole>No, you can't go home. You hit your head.

01:14:08.500 --> 01:14:10.590
<v Sasheer>Yeah, I know. But it's fine.

01:14:10.590 --> 01:14:16.810
<v Nicole>No, it's not fine. We have to go to the hospital. Your face is, like, twice the size it should be.

01:14:16.810 --> 01:14:31.040
<v Sasheer Zamata>We get off the bus, and the cops are already there. I see the car that we hit, and it's a tiny red car. And we T-boned it. Except the T is very disproportionate, where the top part of it's very tiny and the bottom part's a huge bus.

01:14:31.040 --> 01:14:32.980
<v Cop>Did you ladies see what happened?

01:14:32.980 --> 01:14:37.760
<v Sasheer>No, we were sitting at the back of the bus, and we couldn't actually see the traffic.

01:14:37.760 --> 01:14:39.320
<v Cop>OK.

01:14:39.320 --> 01:14:42.360
<v Nicole>But the bus driver did it.

01:14:42.360 --> 01:14:43.950
<v Cop>But you just said you didn't see anything.

01:14:43.950 --> 01:14:52.620
<v Nicole>Yeah, but he said, (IMITATING) I drive crazy-- before the accident, and then he did drive crazy.

01:14:52.620 --> 01:14:55.370
<v Sasheer Zamata>A concept that was still blowing our minds.

01:14:55.370 --> 01:14:57.260
<v Cop>But you didn't see the accident?

01:14:57.260 --> 01:15:06.370
<v Nicole>No, we didn't. But how could  it have been the other guy's fault? We were on the bus that hit him. Also, can someone take my friend to the hospital? She hit her head real hard.

01:15:06.370 --> 01:15:13.130
<v Sasheer Zamata>Nicole was amazing in high-stress situations-- like the time when I told her I was pregnant.

01:15:13.130 --> 01:15:20.160
<v Sasheer>Um, yeah, so, we didn't use a condom. And I haven't had my period in a while.

01:15:20.160 --> 01:15:24.860
<v Nicole>It's OK. This is fine. OK, I support whatever you want.

01:15:24.860 --> 01:15:26.770
<v Sasheer>I don't know what I'm supposed to tell my family.

01:15:26.770 --> 01:15:30.800
<v Nicole>Oh, listen. I will go with you to the clinic, or if you don't want me to, whatever you want.

01:15:30.800 --> 01:15:33.410
<v Sasheer>I just-- I can't have a kid right now.

01:15:33.410 --> 01:15:36.480
<v Nicole>No, no, no. I know. Come here. It's going to be OK. I love you, OK?

01:15:36.480 --> 01:15:36.780
<v Sasheer>OK.

01:15:36.780 --> 01:15:37.270
<v Nicole>I love you.

01:15:37.270 --> 01:15:37.580
<v Sasheer>All right.

01:15:37.580 --> 01:15:38.050
<v Nicole>OK?

01:15:38.050 --> 01:15:52.550
<v Sasheer>OK. Um, I really appreciate this. And-- April Fools!

01:15:52.550 --> 01:15:54.320
<v Nicole>What the fuck?

01:15:54.320 --> 01:15:59.910
<v Nicole>[APPLAUSE]

01:15:59.910 --> 01:16:04.840
<v Nicole>What the fuck is wrong with you?

01:16:04.840 --> 01:16:24.590
<v Sasheer Zamata>See? She's a great friend. And the perfect person to have for this stupid bus accident. We get onto an ambulance and head towards the hospital. And the driver of the other car is already there. And he's much chattier than most would be after getting into a serious accident like this.

01:16:24.590 --> 01:16:26.970
<v Driver Of The Other Car>So you guys were on that bus?

01:16:26.970 --> 01:16:31.200
<v Sasheer>Oh. Uh, yes. We were.

01:16:31.200 --> 01:16:33.840
<v Driver Of The Other Car>It was a rental company bus. Did you rent a car?

01:16:33.840 --> 01:16:36.580
<v Sasheer>Uh, yeah. We rented a car to go to Boston.

01:16:36.580 --> 01:16:40.700
<v Driver Of The Other Car>Oh, cool. What was going on in Boston?

01:16:40.700 --> 01:16:46.470
<v Sasheer>Um, we were there for a festival.

01:16:46.470 --> 01:16:48.680
<v Driver Of The Other Car>What kind of festival?

01:16:48.680 --> 01:16:51.260
<v Sasheer>A comedy festival.

01:16:51.260 --> 01:16:54.700
<v Driver Of The Other Car>Wow! So you're a comedian?

01:16:54.700 --> 01:16:55.530
<v Sasheer>Yes.

01:16:55.530 --> 01:16:57.180
<v Driver Of The Other Car>Does that mean you're a comedian, too?

01:16:57.180 --> 01:17:03.270
<v Nicole>Don't talk to me!

01:17:03.270 --> 01:17:14.210
<v Sasheer Zamata>I don't know why I just didn't do that! Telling a person who's not a comedian that you're a comedian is never good because the conversation that follows is always terrible.

01:17:14.210 --> 01:17:19.950
<v Driver Of The Other Car>So you were up in Boston. Did you make any jokes about baked beans? They love those up there.

01:17:19.950 --> 01:17:22.720
<v Sasheer>No, I didn't make any jokes about baked beans.

01:17:22.720 --> 01:17:31.000
<v Driver Of The Other Car>Listen, next time you're in Boston, you say, (KENNEDYESQUE ACCENT) pahk the cah. It'll kill. It will kill. You can have that one. That one's yours. You can have that for free.

01:17:31.000 --> 01:17:36.400
<v Sasheer>OK, thank you. Thank you so much. I'm gonna stop talking now because my head really hurts.

01:17:36.400 --> 01:17:37.930
<v Driver Of The Other Car>(SNORTING) I get it.

01:17:37.930 --> 01:17:38.840
<v Sasheer>It's not a joke.

01:17:38.840 --> 01:17:41.510
<v Driver Of The Other Car>I get it.

01:17:41.510 --> 01:18:22.980
<v Sasheer Zamata>I don't want to say the name of the hospital that we went to because someone here in the audience may actually work there. But we went to Elmhurst Hospital. And it was the dirtiest hospital I've ever been to in my life. There were Toblerone wrappers on stretcher beds and loose muffins on the floor-- just, like, unwrapped, unexplained, whole muffins.

01:18:22.980 --> 01:18:34.500
<v Sasheer Zamata>I'm left with Nicole in the waiting room. And we're waiting and waiting. Nicole's actually really scared of what would happen to me if I fell asleep. So she makes it her job to make sure I stay awake.

01:18:34.500 --> 01:18:35.350
<v Sasheer>Hm?

01:18:35.350 --> 01:18:35.490
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:35.490 --> 01:18:35.730
<v Sasheer>Yeah?

01:18:35.730 --> 01:18:36.000
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:36.000 --> 01:18:36.920
<v Sasheer>Mm-hmm?

01:18:36.920 --> 01:18:37.200
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:37.200 --> 01:18:38.070
<v Sasheer>Yeah?

01:18:38.070 --> 01:18:39.290
<v Nicole>You see that doctor over there?

01:18:39.290 --> 01:18:40.050
<v Sasheer>Uh-huh.

01:18:40.050 --> 01:18:43.440
<v Nicole>Would you fuck him?

01:18:43.440 --> 01:18:44.660
<v Sasheer>You said he's a doctor, right?

01:18:44.660 --> 01:18:45.070
<v Nicole>Yeah.

01:18:45.070 --> 01:18:48.440
<v Sasheer>So probably, yeah.

01:18:48.440 --> 01:18:49.170
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:49.170 --> 01:18:49.450
<v Sasheer>Yeah?

01:18:49.450 --> 01:18:49.570
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:49.570 --> 01:18:49.840
<v Sasheer>Mm-hmm?

01:18:49.840 --> 01:18:50.290
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:50.290 --> 01:18:51.030
<v Sasheer>Yeah?

01:18:51.030 --> 01:18:51.760
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:51.760 --> 01:18:52.050
<v Sasheer>Yes?

01:18:52.050 --> 01:18:52.340
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:52.340 --> 01:18:52.630
<v Sasheer>Yes?

01:18:52.630 --> 01:18:52.920
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:52.920 --> 01:18:53.140
<v Sasheer>Hm?

01:18:53.140 --> 01:18:53.260
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:53.260 --> 01:18:53.900
<v Sasheer>Yes?

01:18:53.900 --> 01:18:54.340
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:54.340 --> 01:18:54.590
<v Sasheer>Yes?

01:18:54.590 --> 01:18:54.810
<v Nicole>Sasheer?

01:18:54.810 --> 01:18:55.510
<v Sasheer>Yes?

01:18:55.510 --> 01:18:56.240
<v Nicole>Are you hungry?

01:18:56.240 --> 01:18:56.990
<v Sasheer>Always.

01:18:56.990 --> 01:18:57.790
<v Nicole>You want a muffin?

01:18:57.790 --> 01:18:59.230
<v Sasheer>Always.

01:18:59.230 --> 01:19:01.080
<v Sasheer>[APPLAUSE]

01:19:01.080 --> 01:19:02.470
<v Nicole>Go get that muffin.

01:19:02.470 --> 01:19:03.390
<v Sasheer>I'll get that muffin.

01:19:03.390 --> 01:19:04.320
<v Nicole>Get that muffin.

01:19:04.320 --> 01:19:04.560
<v Sasheer>I'm gonna get that muffin.

01:19:04.560 --> 01:19:05.440
<v Nicole>Go get that muffin.

01:19:05.440 --> 01:19:05.880
<v Sasheer>I'm gonna get--

01:19:05.880 --> 01:19:06.760
<v Nicole>Go get that muffin.

01:19:06.760 --> 01:19:07.210
<v Sasheer>I'm gonna get it.

01:19:07.210 --> 01:19:09.210
<v Nicole>Go get that muffin! Go get that muffin!

01:19:09.210 --> 01:19:10.030
<v Sasheer>I got it.

01:19:10.030 --> 01:19:10.940
<v Nicole>You got that muffin!

01:19:10.940 --> 01:19:12.310
<v Sasheer>I got it!

01:19:12.310 --> 01:19:18.410
<v Nicole>Oh, no! No, no, no, no. That's a dirty muffin. Put that muffin down.

01:19:18.410 --> 01:19:19.310
<v Sasheer>[MUMBLING].

01:19:19.310 --> 01:19:22.130
<v Nicole>Oh, no. Maybe eat that candy off the chair.

01:19:22.130 --> 01:19:25.490
<v Sasheer>OK. There's nothing in here.

01:19:25.490 --> 01:19:32.320
<v Nicole>Oh, no! The hot doctor must have eaten it all! He loves chocolate.

01:19:32.320 --> 01:19:34.730
<v Sasheer>Ah. Does that mean he'll love me?

01:19:34.730 --> 01:19:41.960
<v Nicole>You're a big old chocolate woman, so yes! But you gotta stay awake. Doctors hate sleepy bitches.

01:19:41.960 --> 01:19:42.990
<v Sasheer>Yeah.

01:19:42.990 --> 01:19:48.370
<v Sasheer>[APPLAUSE]

01:19:48.370 --> 01:19:49.890
<v Sasheer>I don't want to be a sleepy bitch.

01:19:49.890 --> 01:19:52.630
<v Nicole>Mm-mm. Because you know what they say about sleepy bitches.

01:19:52.630 --> 01:19:53.100
<v Sasheer>What?

01:19:53.100 --> 01:20:00.600
<v Nicole>They too tired!

01:20:00.600 --> 01:20:04.590
<v Sasheer Zamata>Five hours pass, and I still haven't gotten seen.

01:20:04.590 --> 01:20:08.240
<v Sasheer>Excuse me, do you know how much longer before I get seen?

01:20:08.240 --> 01:20:10.850
<v Nurse>What you need to be seen for?

01:20:10.850 --> 01:20:14.700
<v Sasheer>I was just in a car accident. I need someone to check my head.

01:20:14.700 --> 01:20:20.880
<v Nurse>Oh! I didn't know you were in a car accident. We thought you were waiting on somebody. You look fine.

01:20:20.880 --> 01:20:22.950
<v Sasheer>But I hurt!

01:20:22.950 --> 01:20:37.290
<v Sasheer Zamata>Eventually I get moved to the CAT scan area, and the guy running the scan looks like he's 12 years old, and he's, like, not putting me at ease.

01:20:37.290 --> 01:20:40.820
<v Child's Voice>OK. So what happened to you?

01:20:40.820 --> 01:20:43.090
<v Sasheer>I was just in a car accident.

01:20:43.090 --> 01:20:46.340
<v Child's Voice>Oh. I couldn't tell. You look fine.

01:20:46.340 --> 01:20:48.370
<v Sasheer>That's what I hear.

01:20:48.370 --> 01:20:53.580
<v Child's Voice>Whoa! Cool gold shoes! Did you spray-paint those yourself?

01:20:53.580 --> 01:20:54.400
<v Sasheer>I did!

01:20:54.400 --> 01:20:58.870
<v Child's Voice>That's so cool! I want to get this one pair of sneakers with this design that has--

01:20:58.870 --> 01:21:01.100
<v Sasheer>OK, can you just scan me, please?

01:21:01.100 --> 01:21:03.340
<v Sasheer>[MACHINE WHIRRING]

01:21:03.340 --> 01:21:13.200
<v Sasheer Zamata>He finally scans me. And then I wait awhile for the results.

01:21:13.200 --> 01:21:17.530
<v Nurse>You're fine.

01:21:17.530 --> 01:21:33.980
<v Sasheer Zamata>Every person involved with this accident was awful. Except for Nicole. She was the best part of this whole thing. So ladies and gentlemen, I'd like to present to you the last best person in New York State, Nicole Byer!

01:21:33.980 --> 01:21:36.950
<v Sasheer Zamata>[FANFARE]

01:21:36.950 --> 01:21:49.300
<v Ira Glass>When God comes back and says if there's one just person, one righteous person, I will not destroy the world-- oh, um, OK. OK, so I will just not--

01:21:49.300 --> 01:21:56.640
<v Ira Glass>It's Ira. Uh, radio listeners, internet listeners, what's happening now onstage is that people are running on giving Nicole flowers and cheering her.

01:21:56.640 --> 01:22:05.660
<v Sasheer Zamata>OK. That's-- that's enough. All right, I still-- I hit my head really hard in the accident.

01:22:05.660 --> 01:22:10.190
<v Sasheer Zamata>OK. Well, thanks a lot for listening to my--

01:22:10.190 --> 01:22:11.150
<v Sasheer Zamata>[LIGHTS   TURNING OFF]

01:22:11.150 --> 01:22:13.560
<v Ira Glass>--story.

01:22:13.560 --> 01:22:23.220
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

01:22:23.220 --> 01:22:44.660
<v Ira Glass>There was some artistic license taken with actual dialogue. The dialogue in that story at the hospital was not reported verbatim. We contacted Elmhurst Hospital about the conditions Sasheer saw, asked for a comment. The spokesperson said she'd get back to us this Wednesday. Didn't get around to it. We reached out a few more times, and we never heard back.

01:22:44.660 --> 01:22:56.440
<v Ira Glass>Sasheer Zamata with Nicole Byer, Chris Gethard, Frank Garcia Hejl, Tony Ward, Zakiya Young, Seth Barrish, and 12-year-old Matthew Mindler as the CAT scan guy.

01:22:56.440 --> 01:23:02.240
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

01:23:02.240 --> 01:23:19.360
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by myself with Robyn Semien and Robert Saenz de Viteri, with our senior producer, Julie Snyder, Alison Davis, and Elise Bergerson, as well as Alex Blumberg, Ben Calhoun, Sean Cole, Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, Sarah Koenig, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Brian Reed, Alissa Shipp, Nancy Updike.

01:23:19.360 --> 01:23:46.410
<v Ira Glass>Seth Lind is our operations director. Emily Condon's our production manager. Adrianne Mathiowetz runs our website. Research help from Michelle Harris and Julie Beer. Some music in today's show by Roger Neill. Thanks to Laura Coburn. Thanks to all the theater professionals who took their time out from their day jobs to be here with us for a week and to be here today-- singers, actors, dancers, writers, composers, directors, and some actual famous people.

01:23:46.410 --> 01:23:47.900
<v Ira Glass>And especially thanks to the core production team led by Robert Saenz de Viteri, Christine Jones set designer, Andrea Lauer costume design, Mark Bartun lighting design, Nevin Steinberg sound design, Ann McPherson stage manager, the BAM crew, they all had to use incredible I have to say cunning and craft to get a show this complicated into a theater and tech it in in one day. We got here this morning.

01:23:47.900 --> 01:24:13.990
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]

01:24:13.990 --> 01:24:31.450
<v Ira Glass>Our website, where you can see everything that was on today's show, the sets, the costumes, the dancing, and, like, seriously half an hour of incredible material that was simply too long to include on the radio, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.

01:24:31.450 --> 01:24:55.150
<v Ira Glass>Thanks, as always, to our show's co-founder, Torey Malatia. I will never forget the crazy birthday party where he ate slice after slice of German chocolate cake. And we kept telling him, Torey, stop with the German chocolate cake. Stop it. You're going to make yourself sick. And we were right. He made himself sick. He was green and sick and then couldn't stop saying--

01:24:55.150 --> 01:25:01.060
<v Carin Gilfry>[SPEAKING GERMAN]

01:25:01.060 --> 01:25:06.470
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life .

01:25:06.470 --> 01:25:19.800
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]

01:25:19.800 --> 01:25:24.800
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
